from reportlab.platypus import SimpleDocTemplate, Paragraph, Spacer, KeepTogether, PageBreak
from reportlab.lib.styles import getSampleStyleSheet, ParagraphStyle
from reportlab.lib import colors
from reportlab.lib.enums import TA_LEFT
from pypdf import PdfReader

OUT='output/pdf/rook-wiki-structure.pdf'
base='https://app.notion.com/p/'
sections=[
('Company','3eac8516f5018120af01caaedd4b0761','This section is the navigation hub for understanding the fictional Rook business, its products, people, shipped work, and plans. It contains four reference pages - About Rook, Rook Dispatch, Rook Supply, and Glossary - and three databases: Team directory, Releases, and Q3 roadmap.'),
('About Rook','3eac8516f50181afbc4de9d8ff334082','This onboarding page introduces Rook as a subscription software business serving independent masked responders, handlers, and quartermasters. Revised in July 2026, it covers company history, locations, staffing, pricing per active responder, the two products, monthly releases, support escalation, and confidentiality rules. Responders are customers rather than employees, and Rook does not store mappings to their legal identities.'),
('Rook Dispatch','3eac8516f5018150bcc2cabf6a6e5e86','This product overview explains how Dispatch coordinates incident response through a handler web console and responder phone app. It describes incident intake, responder ranking, sequential pings, acceptance, and assignment; outlines each user\'s responsibilities; and defines acceptance rate, time-to-accept, and coverage gaps as key measures. Routing configuration ships with monthly releases rather than being adjustable by handlers.'),
('Rook Supply','3eac8516f5018117b74de4d0d0b17cf2','This product overview describes equipment provisioning and maintenance for responders. Its workflow covers handler requisitions, quartermaster approval, fulfillment, equipment service intervals, and field failure reports that can trigger earlier maintenance. Supply reads the Responder Availability Record maintained by Dispatch to schedule maintenance, so Dispatch changes can affect Supply without a separate Supply release.'),
('Glossary','3eac8516f50181f78bd1eb9da42687bb','This reference page establishes shared terminology across people, callouts, routing, and equipment provisioning. Maintained by Product Management and last updated on 4 August 2026, it distinguishes callouts from pings, explicit turn-downs from missed responses, and low acceptance from coverage gaps. It also defines routing inputs, capability tags, the shared availability record, mutual aid, requisitions, failure reports, and service intervals.'),
('Team directory','9b195a82494e4a98aa961149cb7205df','This database identifies seven Dispatch contacts across product, engineering, design, support, and data. Its properties are Name (title), Role, Location, and Owns (text). Entries include Helen Achebe, Marcus Oyelaran, Wen Li, Sofia Marino, Ravi Menon, Nadia Hoffmann, and departed PM Priya Raghunathan. Last updated on 2 September 2026, the individual entry pages contain no additional body content.'),
('Releases','2fafeca82e2142d288fdb00c81b1bf89','This database records published Dispatch release notes using Release (title), Ship date (date), and What changed (text). Its entries cover 4.0\'s navigation, profiles, and override audit log; 4.1\'s travel-time proximity, bulk callouts, and push improvements; and 4.2\'s routing weights, shorter ping wait, persistent filters, and defect fixes. Each entry contains detailed notes; 4.2 also has discussion of elevated support complaints and unresolved questions. Deferred work is excluded.'),
('Q3 roadmap','950ebd7431764f11b6dc1cc13378dca2','This planning database tracks six Dispatch and Supply initiatives using Item (title), Target release, Owner, Status (select), and Last reviewed (date). It lists routing changes, Availability Confidence, ping timeout tuning, and requisition approval chains as committed, with the handler phone app and shared cover exploring for Q4. Every entry was last reviewed on 30 June 2026, so these are dated plans rather than verified delivery status. Availability Confidence is listed against 4.2 but absent from its release notes.'),
('Research','3eac8516f501813ab20bdb8ea3d37b28','This section provides a home for research with Rook users. Its only listed child is the Customer interviews database, which holds September 2026 console redesign interview records. The parent page contains a short introduction and does not provide a separate research synthesis or additional research collections.'),
('Customer interviews','83a83da875364a1ab725d914482fad35','This database contains four interview pages for Aunt Dot, Mr. Ambrose, Halloran, and Kip, with Interview (title), Date (date), Handler, Interviewer, and Responder (text) properties. Transcript excerpts describe working conditions and concerns such as small text, filter resets, dark mode, notification visibility, uneven callout distribution, short response windows, and Supply approval delays. These are participant accounts rather than verified explanations of product behavior; complete recordings are not provided in the available content.'),
('Product briefs','3eac8516f50181879cfaf12bd7bbf43c','This section collects one-page proposals for individual features. Its four child pages are Bulk Callout, Handler Phone App, Requisition Approval Chains, and Routing Override Audit Log. Their headings vary but cover proposals, problems, ownership, scope, success measures, and next steps or open questions. The briefs preserve proposal context; delivery status must be checked against release records.'),
('Bulk Callout','3eac8516f50181b5bf1efe6be35d119a','Dated 12 January 2026, this brief proposes letting handlers send the same callout to several responders in one action while preserving individual acceptance and decline responses. Sections are Problem, Proposal, Success measure, Scope, and Open questions. It targets less reliance on direct phone calls and faster full coverage, without an established baseline. Group chat, bulk requisitions, and ranking changes are excluded. Although implementation questions remain in the brief, release 4.1 subsequently lists bulk callout as shipped.'),
('Handler Phone App','3eac8516f50181b9b6c9fa532191342f','Dated 8 September 2026, this brief proposes push notifications, live responder status, and one-tap calling or messaging for handlers. Sections are Proposal, What this solves, Owner, Success measure, and Scope, linking the idea to a handler\'s request for awareness away from the console. The product designer owns Q4 exploration, no build team is staffed, and no measurement baseline exists. Supply features and broader console functionality are outside its stated scope.'),
('Requisition Approval Chains','3eac8516f50181bd80c0fbde07980760','Dated 14 July 2026, this Supply brief proposes a manager\'s second approval after quartermaster approval for requisitions over $2,000. Sections are Problem, Proposal, Owner, Success measure, and Open questions; success means zero qualifying shipments without the second sign-off. It also introduces rerouting after 48 hours, approval dashboards, handler status visibility, and possible spreadsheet replacement, but leaves which additions ship together or in phases unresolved.'),
('Routing Override Audit Log','3eac8516f50181bf80eff897b294a174','Dated 5 March 2026, this Dispatch brief proposes recording manual routing overrides so Support can investigate why a recommended responder was passed over. Sections are Problem, Proposal, Owner, Scope, and Next steps. It specifies the handler, time, selected responder, and optional reason in the console activity view. Engineering owns implementation, design mockups were available, and scope is limited to overrides. Release 4.0 subsequently lists the audit log as shipped.')]
styles=getSampleStyleSheet()
styles.add(ParagraphStyle(name='TitleX',fontName='Helvetica-Bold',fontSize=25,leading=29,textColor=colors.HexColor('#15384A'),spaceAfter=10))
styles.add(ParagraphStyle(name='IntroX',fontSize=10,leading=14,textColor=colors.HexColor('#52616B'),spaceAfter=20))
styles.add(ParagraphStyle(name='HeadingX',fontName='Helvetica-Bold',fontSize=12,leading=16,textColor=colors.HexColor('#15384A'),spaceAfter=5))
styles.add(ParagraphStyle(name='BodyX',fontSize=10,leading=14,spaceAfter=15))
doc=SimpleDocTemplate(OUT,pagesize=(612,792),rightMargin=48,leftMargin=48,topMargin=47,bottomMargin=46,title='Rook Wiki: Structure and Content',author='Course workspace')
story=[Paragraph('Rook Wiki<br/>Structure and Content',styles['TitleX']),Paragraph('A guide to the connected Notion wiki | 29 October 2026<br/>Fictional teaching scenario. Each element title links to its source page or database. Dates and statuses reflect the source records.',styles['IntroX'])]
for title,id,body in sections:
    story.append(KeepTogether([Paragraph(f'<link href="{base+id}" color="#176C88">{title}</link>',styles['HeadingX']),Paragraph(body,styles['BodyX'])]))
def footer(c,d):
    c.setStrokeColor(colors.HexColor('#D8E2E7'));c.line(48,35,564,35)
    c.setFont('Helvetica',8);c.setFillColor(colors.HexColor('#52616B'));c.drawString(48,23,'ROOK INDUSTRIES | Fictional course wiki');c.drawRightString(564,23,str(d.page))
doc.build(story,onFirstPage=footer,onLaterPages=footer)
r=PdfReader(OUT)
print('Pages:',len(r.pages),'Links:',sum(len(p.get('/Annots',[])) for p in r.pages))
for i,p in enumerate(r.pages): print(i+1,len(p.extract_text()),p.extract_text()[:65])
